WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:08.104
A report adds up every payment above 9,000 cents, out of 10 million payments.

2
00:00:08.333 --> 00:00:14.397
Checking one amount takes the processor well under a nanosecond, a billionth of a second.

3
00:00:14.397 --> 00:00:18.757
Written as one plain loop, the whole job takes 3.3 milliseconds.

4
00:00:18.933 --> 00:00:25.253
Run the usual way, one row at a time through a database's plan, it takes 139.

5
00:00:25.253 --> 00:00:28.701
So where do the other 136 milliseconds go?

6
00:00:28.701 --> 00:00:30.260
Hold that question.

7
00:00:30.533 --> 00:00:32.472
Start in a second-hand bookshop.

8
00:00:32.472 --> 00:00:37.501
You're at the counter, and a friend fetches novels priced over 5 for you to add up.

9
00:00:37.700 --> 00:00:39.364
You say: one more, please.

10
00:00:39.364 --> 00:00:44.355
Your friend walks along the shelves, checks a price, and brings back one book.

11
00:00:44.355 --> 00:00:45.571
Then you ask again.

12
00:00:46.300 --> 00:00:49.133
A database runs our report the same way.

13
00:00:49.133 --> 00:00:54.870
Before it runs, the query becomes a plan, a tree of small steps called operators.

14
00:00:54.870 --> 00:01:00.891
A scan reads the payments, a filter keeps amounts over 9,000, and a sum adds them up.

15
00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:05.777
Each operator gets its input by asking the one below it for the next row.

16
00:01:05.777 --> 00:01:08.395
That ask is a function call, named next.

17
00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:15.668
So the sum is you at the counter, the filter is your friend checking the price, and the scan is the walk along the shelves.

18
00:01:15.867 --> 00:01:17.124
Watch one row.

19
00:01:17.124 --> 00:01:20.448
The scan reads 4,200 and hands it up.

20
00:01:20.448 --> 00:01:23.591
It fails, so the filter asks again.

21
00:01:23.591 --> 00:01:26.645
9,700 passes, and the sum adds it.

22
00:01:26.900 --> 00:01:29.947
Then the whole chain runs again for the next row.

23
00:01:29.947 --> 00:01:32.311
Every row costs a call at every level.

24
00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:37.327
This is the iterator model, and PostgreSQL runs queries this way.

25
00:01:37.327 --> 00:01:41.455
For our report, the scan alone is called 10 million times.

26
00:01:41.455 --> 00:01:45.797
The query takes 139 milliseconds, about 14 nanoseconds a row.

27
00:01:46.033 --> 00:01:48.398
So, the other 136 milliseconds.

28
00:01:48.398 --> 00:01:52.058
You might think the comparison is the slow part.

29
00:01:52.058 --> 00:01:52.745
It isn't.

30
00:01:52.745 --> 00:01:56.634
The plain loop needs a third of a nanosecond a row.

31
00:01:56.634 --> 00:01:58.312
The rest is machinery.

32
00:01:58.500 --> 00:02:03.645
Every call goes through the same door, so the chip can't guess which code comes next.

33
00:02:03.645 --> 00:02:09.820
And the condition is worked out by walking a little tree, one node for each part of amount over 9,000.

34
00:02:10.033 --> 00:02:17.370
Researchers in Amsterdam measured this in 2005, on MySQL, a popular free database program.

35
00:02:17.370 --> 00:02:22.505
A standard report over 6 million order lines took 26.6 seconds.

36
00:02:22.733 --> 00:02:27.116
The additions and multiplications were under a tenth of it.

37
00:02:27.116 --> 00:02:30.384
A program written by hand took 0.22 seconds.

38
00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:34.820
Your friend spends the afternoon walking, and about a minute reading prices.

39
00:02:34.820 --> 00:02:38.485
Wouldn't it be nice if every trip brought back more than one book?

40
00:02:38.767 --> 00:02:41.294
So send your friend with a crate.

41
00:02:41.294 --> 00:02:50.023
Vectorized execution changes what next returns: a batch of 1,024 amounts, all from one column, in one plain array.

42
00:02:50.200 --> 00:02:54.541
The filter runs one tight loop over the array, with no calls inside.

43
00:02:54.541 --> 00:03:01.499
The chip can overlap many rows at once, and the compiler can even compare several amounts in one instruction.

44
00:03:01.667 --> 00:03:04.039
A call is now paid once per crate.

45
00:03:04.039 --> 00:03:07.248
That's under 10,000 calls for 10 million rows.

46
00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:10.086
So how much faster is the report now?

47
00:03:10.086 --> 00:03:11.204
Pause and guess.

48
00:03:14.467 --> 00:03:20.084
18.8 milliseconds, about 7 times faster than 139.

49
00:03:20.300 --> 00:03:29.561
In 2005, the Amsterdam team's system, X100, ran its report in half a second, over 50 times faster than MySQL.

50
00:03:29.561 --> 00:03:31.940
Same books, far fewer trips.

51
00:03:32.200 --> 00:03:34.400
So, bigger crates, always better?

52
00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:35.867
Let's be fair and try.

53
00:03:35.867 --> 00:03:39.601
One value per batch brings the call overhead right back.

54
00:03:39.833 --> 00:03:42.965
Around 1,000 was fastest in X100's tests.

55
00:03:42.965 --> 00:03:52.284
Above about 8,000, the batch no longer fits in the CPU cache, the small, fast memory on the chip, and it slows down again.

56
00:03:52.467 --> 00:03:56.527
The crate no longer fits on the counter, so the books go on the floor.

57
00:03:56.800 --> 00:04:04.295
DuckDB, a free database program built for reports, runs every query in batches of 2,048.

58
00:04:04.500 --> 00:04:06.808
PostgreSQL keeps one row at a time.

59
00:04:06.808 --> 00:04:15.116
Its typical query finds a few rows through an index, where the cost per row is noise, and small operators are easy to combine.

60
00:04:15.333 --> 00:04:19.191
So the moral of the story: passing rows costs a call each time.

61
00:04:19.191 --> 00:04:26.845
Pass one row per call when there are few rows, and a crate per call when there are millions, so the time goes to the amounts.

62
00:04:27.067 --> 00:04:28.314
One quick check.

63
00:04:28.314 --> 00:04:32.054
A support screen finds 10 rows through an index.

64
00:04:32.054 --> 00:04:35.873
Would batches of 1,024 make it noticeably faster?

65
00:04:35.873 --> 00:04:37.120
Pause and think.

66
00:04:40.333 --> 00:04:40.817
Hardly.

67
00:04:40.817 --> 00:04:43.927
Ten rows of overhead is too little to notice.

68
00:04:43.927 --> 00:04:47.521
Batches pay off when a query reads millions of rows.
